-- A raw `__fastfail` / `int 29h` still cannot be caught in-process; for those the
-  Wine log via `PROTON_LOG=1` remains the source of truth.
+- A raw `__fastfail` / `int 29h` still cannot be intercepted reliably in-process.
+  v0.2 can now detect the resulting unclean session on the next launch and correlate
+  REDengine ReportQueue artifacts, but the Wine log via `PROTON_LOG=1` remains an
+  important source for the actual low-level termination path.
+
+## v0.2 — additional crash detection
+
+This revision adds several paths that the original in-process exception handler could not diagnose well:
+
+- **Previous-session postmortem recovery.** Every run owns `CrashLogger/sessions/<pid>.active`. A clean exit removes it. If it survives, the next launch creates `postmortem_<timestamp>_pid<pid>.log` and correlates the dead session with the newest `%LOCALAPPDATA%\REDEngine\ReportQueue` folder, embedding `stacktrace.txt` and `CrashInfo.json` when present. This gives evidence for raw fail-fast, driver/process kills and crashes that die before the rich writer finishes.
+- **Resource pressure watchdog.** Every five seconds it records system commit, available physical RAM, process private/working-set memory, handle count and free log-volume space. Threshold crossings are flushed to `CrashLogger_trace.log`; crash and hang reports contain last/peak/minimum values.
+- **All-thread snapshots.** Crash reports now unwind up to 48 non-crashing threads (24 frames each). Hang reports capture up to 32 other threads (16 frames each). Threads with a RED4ext/ASI/loader DLL on their stack are marked, which makes cross-thread deadlocks and worker failures much easier to identify.
+- **More fatal runtime paths.** IAT hooks now also catch imported `abort()` and `_purecall()`, and a non-zero `ExitProcess`. The VEH guard recognizes heap corruption (`0xC0000374`), stack-buffer/fail-fast (`0xC0000409`), no-memory (`0xC0000017`), insufficient-resources (`0xC000009A`) and fatal-user-callback (`0xC000041D`) failures.
+- **Crash-class hints.** Reports label resource exhaustion, heap corruption, stack exhaustion, runtime abort, pure virtual calls, `std::terminate`, self-termination and non-zero process exit so the raw exception code does not need to be decoded manually.
+
+The previous-session recovery intentionally treats a surviving marker as **abnormal termination**, not proof of a specific root cause. The correlated REDengine artifacts, trace breadcrumbs, resource history and stacks are the evidence used to narrow the cause.
